t(6, 865, """If the Kingdom of Morocco were blessed with light rain falling daily without interruption for 365 days, this scenario would be considered "the ideal environmental and hydrological dream" for reviving the aquifers.

Scientifically, light and continuous rain is the best and most efficient way to feed groundwater reservoirs, the exact opposite of heavy torrential rain, which causes floods and is lost to the sea.

When rain falls slowly and lightly, the soil has enough time to absorb it fully without becoming saturated quickly.

The result: Moroccan land will turn into a "giant sponge". Water will seep through the soil pores and rock cracks to reach the depths of the earth, refilling shallow and deep reservoirs at an efficiency that may reach 100%.

Abandoned wells: wells that dried up in recent years in areas such as the Souss basin, Al Haouz and Sais will see a rapid rise of water, bringing it close to the surface.

Natural springs gushing: water springs and historic khettaras (traditional underground irrigation channels) that had dried up will flow again in the oases of Tafilalet and the Atlas regions.

Deep geological layers (such as Liassic layers beneath the Atlas Mountains) will receive continuous recharge they have not seen for decades.

This rainy year will form a "strategic security stock" sufficient for Morocco for long years to come, protecting it from any sudden future waves of drought.

Since the rain continues daily, farmers will completely stop running well pumps to irrigate crops, because the falling rain will do the job for free.

This collective halt in pumping will give the aquifers a unique "biological rest period", allowing them to recover and regain their natural balance and lost hydraulic pressure.

In coastal cities (such as the mouths of the Souss, Moulouya and Doukkala basins), when the groundwater level drops, salty sea water seeps in and contaminates the freshwater reservoirs.

With this continuous rainfall, the pressure of fresh groundwater will extend outward, driving out the sea water and protecting coastal farmland from salinisation for good.

The year will end with Morocco holding the strongest and richest groundwater reserve in its modern history, with complete greening of the plant cover, which will work together with this water to stabilise the soil, putting the Kingdom's water system completely safe from climate change.""")

h(7, 350, """The truth is that the groundwater stock in the Kingdom of Morocco is suffering severely! But!""")

