t(7, 1960, """Every enormous human mass imposes its collective will in the world's dealings with it, since it is fragile when it disintegrates and splinters: then the strong world deals with each of its disintegrated components separately, and so the strong world has its own way in its method and style of imposing its will. If a nation splinters, the strong, advanced world imposes its terms and policies on it and makes the splintered components consumers of its products. But when the strong world deals with the united will of a nation, that nation of enormous numbers can impose a partnership in manufacturing, not assembly, on its own land. It is then no longer hostage to manufacturing coming from outside its land, nor ignorant of the ways of manufacturing; it does not accept the worthless partnership of assembly, but imposes its will east and west for a partnership in real manufacturing, and shapes the policy of global opportunities with confidence and strength. If the West refuses, the East accepts; if the East refuses, the West accepts; and if both refuse, the South or the North cooperates!""")

